  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 202 AM EDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nA strong ridge pattern across the western US will continue to expand \nacross to even the southeastern US while surface high pressure \nremains parked over the Florida Peninsula. This pattern will prevent \ntroughing across most of the Eastern Seaboard from pushing further \nsouth, which is expected to keep the local weather much quieter than \nnormal through Thursday. \n\nAs we move into Friday, a westward moving upper-level trough is \nexpected to advect across Cuba and parts of the Florida Straits for \nFriday and into the weekend. Long range guidance seems to be better \naligned at this point with expecting this trough to avoid \nstrengthening into a tropical system. Ensembles do not highlight \nthis feature forming any low level circulation, giving increased \nconfidence that the only real impact will be increasing rain chances \nas deeper moisture returns to the region for Friday and beyond into \nthe end of the forecast period. Enhanced troughing across the \nEastern Seaboard is also being hinted at for the end of the period \non Sunday through early next week, with some positive vorticity \nadvection potentially reaching South Florida and providing extra \nconvection support in addition to the daily sea breeze circulations. \nHowever, high uncertainty exists given this is near the end of the \nforecast period. \n\nHigh temperatures each day will continue to reach the low to mid \n90s. Overnight lows will range from the low 70s for interior South \nFlorida to the upper 70s and low 80s near the Gulf and Atlantic \ncoasts. Potential for Heat Advisories will increase again late this \nweek and into the weekend as elevated moisture returns to the area \nthat could create enough of a humidity rise to necessitate excessive \nheat products.",
